<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch07_P04_Structure_Of_Divisibility_Proof</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Here is the induction step of the proof that \(f(n) = 4^n + 15n - 1\) is a multiple of 9, in numbered lines. The lemma is the result proved by the second induction: \(3 \times 4^j + 15\) is a multiple of 9 for every positive integer \(j\).</p>
<table>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Assume that \(4^k + 15k - 1 = 9m\) for some integer \(m\).</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Then \(4^{k+1} + 15(k+1) - 1 = 4 \times 4^k + 15k + 15 - 1\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>\(= 4^k + 3 \times 4^k + 15k + 15 - 1\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>\(= \left(4^k + 15k - 1\right) + 3 \times 4^k + 15\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>\(= 9m + 3 \times 4^k + 15\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>\(= 9m + 9s\) for some integer \(s\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>\(= 9(m + s)\),</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>so \(4^{k+1} + 15(k+1) - 1\) is a multiple of 9.</td></tr>
</table>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>In which line is the induction hypothesis (line 1) used?</p>
<div>Line [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
<li><p>In which line is the lemma used?</p>
<div>Line [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
<li><p>Which is the best summary of the whole proof?</p>
<div>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>The proof has two modules: the main induction on \(n\), and the lemma, proved by an induction of its own. Each hypothesis enters at one line.</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Line 4 only regroups the terms so that \(4^k + 15k - 1\) appears. Line 5 uses the induction hypothesis of the main proof, replacing \(4^k + 15k - 1\) by \(9m\).</p></li>
<li><p>Line 6 uses the lemma with \(j = k\), replacing \(3 \times 4^k + 15\) by \(9s\).</p></li>
<li><p>In summary: write \(f(k+1)\) as \(f(k) + 3 \times 4^k + 15\). The first part is a multiple of 9 by the induction hypothesis, and the second is a multiple of 9 by the lemma, a second induction. Checking a few values is evidence, not proof; assuming the result for every \(n\) would be circular; and the terms of \(f(k+1)\) are not multiples of 9 one by one, since \(4^{k+1}\) never is.</p></li>
</ol>]]></text></generalfeedback>
